WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.997 --> 00:00:16.961
The Mediterranean Sea is bordered by orange trees and rugged mountains.

2
00:00:18.002 --> 00:00:21.163
The air is pure and perfumed with the scent of myrtle.

3
00:00:22.123 --> 00:00:26.585
But Captain Nemo was in a hurry, so I experienced none of the Mediterranean's enchantments.

4
00:00:27.706 --> 00:00:33.368
Starting from the shores of Greece on the morning of February 16, we traveled at full speed and

5
00:00:33.408 --> 00:00:35.670
reached the Atlantic Ocean 48 hours later.

6
00:00:36.611 --> 00:00:40.934
It was obvious to me that Captain Nemo wanted to avoid the countries surrounding the Mediterranean.

7
00:00:41.934 --> 00:00:46.258
The breeze probably brought back too many memories, and perhaps too many regrets.

8
00:00:47.358 --> 00:00:51.061
We were traveling so quickly that Ned had to give up any hope of escape.

9
00:00:52.062 --> 00:00:56.445
It would have been impossible to launch the small boat when we were traveling thirty-five to forty feet

10
00:00:56.505 --> 00:00:57.005
per second.

11
00:00:57.966 --> 00:01:00.348
He might as well have jumped off a train going at full speed.

12
00:01:01.268 --> 00:01:06.473
I followed our route on the gallery map and calculated that we had traveled ten thousand leagues in

13
00:01:06.573 --> 00:01:07.534
three and a half months.

14
00:01:08.955 --> 00:01:13.839
Later in the morning of February 18, the Nautilus rose to the surface, so we could take our

15
00:01:13.879 --> 00:01:15.241
daily walk upon the platform.

16
00:01:16.302 --> 00:01:19.004
I went up at once, accompanied by Ned and Conseil.

17
00:01:20.085 --> 00:01:25.250
But the Atlantic Ocean was so rough that the submarine rocked violently with every wave that crashed over

18
00:01:25.310 --> 00:01:27.592
it. We soon decided to go below.

19
00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:31.403
Conseil returned to his cabin while Ned followed me to mine.

20
00:01:32.404 --> 00:01:35.486
When my door was shut, he sat down and looked at me silently.

21
00:01:36.567 --> 00:01:41.652
"Ned, my friend," I said, "I know how disappointed you must be that we couldn't escape while in

22
00:01:41.672 --> 00:01:42.412
the Mediterranean."

23
00:01:43.393 --> 00:01:48.378
The harpooner did not respond, yet the crease in his brow and the expression on his face showed

24
00:01:48.418 --> 00:01:49.699
me that he was thinking hard.

25
00:01:49.719 --> 00:01:53.222
"I don't think we should give up yet," I continued.

26
00:01:53.882 --> 00:01:55.504
"We're going up the coast of Portugal.

27
00:01:56.840 --> 00:02:01.483
France and England are not far away, and we can easily find refuge in one of those countries.

28
00:02:02.583 --> 00:02:07.146
Now if the Nautilus were traveling south, I'd share your uneasiness, but I think you'll be able to

29
00:02:07.326 --> 00:02:08.187
act in a few days."

30
00:02:09.287 --> 00:02:12.129
Ned stared at me for a few more moments before he spoke.

31
00:02:12.149 --> 00:02:13.950
"We're leaving tonight."

32
00:02:15.011 --> 00:02:17.712
I was so startled by this statement that words failed me.

33
00:02:17.732 --> 00:02:23.676
"We agreed to wait for an opportunity," Ned went on, "and that opportunity has arrived.

34
00:02:24.064 --> 00:02:26.965
Tonight we'll be only a few miles from the Spanish coast.

35
00:02:27.326 --> 00:02:30.167
It's cloudy and windy—good conditions for an escape."

36
00:02:31.407 --> 00:02:34.229
As I was still silent, Ned stood up and faced me.

37
00:02:34.249 --> 00:02:37.490
"We leave tonight at nine o'clock," he said.

38
00:02:37.510 --> 00:02:39.711
"I've already told Conseil to be ready.

39
00:02:41.252 --> 00:02:44.353
At that time Captain Nemo will be in his room, probably asleep.

40
00:02:45.354 --> 00:02:50.036
Conseil and I will go up the central staircase, and you'll remain nearby in the library, awaiting my

41
00:02:50.096 --> 00:02:55.946
signal. The oars, mast, and sail are already in the small boat, and I've even gotten us some

42
00:02:55.966 --> 00:02:58.307
provisions. So everything is ready."

43
00:02:58.327 --> 00:03:03.349
"But the sea is so rough," I said, finally finding my voice.

44
00:03:03.369 --> 00:03:08.551
"I agree, but we have a sturdy boat and we'll be traveling only a few miles.

45
00:03:09.611 --> 00:03:11.512
Who knows where the Nautilus will be tomorrow?

46
00:03:12.493 --> 00:03:16.374
We could be hundreds of leagues away, so we have to take this chance tonight.

47
00:03:17.234 --> 00:03:19.135
I will see you later, at nine o'clock."

48
00:03:20.156 --> 00:03:23.561
With these words Ned departed, leaving me feeling numb.

49
00:03:24.601 --> 00:03:29.423
I had always imagined that when the time came to escape, there would be an opportunity to discuss

50
00:03:29.483 --> 00:03:34.505
it. But Ned had given me no time to think, never mind talk, over the matter.

51
00:03:35.526 --> 00:03:38.447
I knew he was right; this was a good opportunity to escape.

52
00:03:39.367 --> 00:03:41.648
But it was still hard to believe that the moment had arrived.

53
00:03:42.649 --> 00:03:46.370
At that point I heard the sounds that meant the submarine was sinking under the waves.

54
00:03:47.431 --> 00:03:52.220
I passed the rest of the day torn between the thrill of freedom and the sadness of abandoning

55
00:03:52.240 --> 00:03:53.281
my underwater studies.

56
00:03:54.341 --> 00:04:00.163
Sometimes I hoped our escape would succeed, and other times I wished something would happen to prevent us

57
00:04:00.223 --> 00:04:01.303
from leaving the submarine.

58
00:04:02.403 --> 00:04:05.204
Twice I went to the gallery to check if we had changed our course.

59
00:04:05.924 --> 00:04:06.324
But no.

60
00:04:07.365 --> 00:04:09.845
The Nautilus was still cruising up the coast of Portugal.

61
00:04:10.586 --> 00:04:12.786
This day of waiting seemed to last forever.

62
00:04:13.786 --> 00:04:16.607
My supper was served in my room as usual, but I could eat little.

63
00:04:18.068 --> 00:04:21.390
At seven o'clock, there were still 120 minutes left.

64
00:04:22.130 --> 00:04:26.133
I couldn't sit quietly anymore so I went to see the gallery for the last time.

65
00:04:27.274 --> 00:04:31.997
I spent a long while admiring its treasures, and I wished the panels were open so I could

66
00:04:32.057 --> 00:04:33.598
gaze underwater one more time.

67
00:04:34.719 --> 00:04:36.640
A clock struck eight, startling me.

68
00:04:37.481 --> 00:04:39.222
I returned to my room and dressed warmly.

69
00:04:40.062 --> 00:04:42.284
I wasn't taking anything with me besides my notes.

70
00:04:43.385 --> 00:04:47.567
At a few minutes before nine, I pressed my ear to the door that linked my cabin and

71
00:04:47.587 --> 00:04:48.128
the captain's.

72
00:04:49.028 --> 00:04:52.650
Silence. I returned to the gallery and then went to the library.

73
00:04:53.711 --> 00:04:57.293
I stood at the door leading to the central staircase and awaited Ned's signal.

74
00:04:58.273 --> 00:05:01.795
Suddenly I heard the submarine's propeller slow down and stop.

75
00:05:02.495 --> 00:05:05.077
The only sound came from my heart beating loudly.

76
00:05:05.977 --> 00:05:10.240
I felt a slight shock and knew that the Nautilus had stopped on the sea bottom.

77
00:05:11.460 --> 00:05:14.162
Something was not right and there was no signal from Ned.

78
00:05:14.182 --> 00:05:15.743
"There you are!"

79
00:05:16.163 --> 00:05:18.224
Captain Nemo entered the library from the gallery.

80
00:05:19.648 --> 00:05:20.809
He was smiling broadly.

81
00:05:20.829 --> 00:05:23.109
"I've been looking all over for you, Professor.

82
00:05:23.990 --> 00:05:25.330
Do you know the history of Spain?"

83
00:05:25.350 --> 00:05:30.472
"What?" I was still startled by the captain's arrival in the midst of my escape.

84
00:05:31.633 --> 00:05:33.354
"I asked if you knew the history of Spain."

85
00:05:34.414 --> 00:05:39.876
Captain Nemo went on to describe a famous sea battle where Spanish ships, loaded with silver and gold,

86
00:05:39.936 --> 00:05:41.477
were sunk in 1702.

87
00:05:41.497 --> 00:05:44.158
"We're right at the shipwreck site.

88
00:05:44.938 --> 00:05:48.540
If you come with me, you'll likely be very interested in what I have to show you."

89
00:05:49.504 --> 00:05:54.525
By this point, I'd recovered my composure, so I followed him into the gallery, where the panels were

90
00:05:54.565 --> 00:05:55.046
now open.

91
00:05:55.806 --> 00:05:57.706
I looked out and saw a shipwreck.

92
00:05:58.747 --> 00:06:03.968
Members of the submarine's crew were carrying silver and gold bars as well as precious jewels back to

93
00:06:04.008 --> 00:06:04.528
the Nautilus.

94
00:06:04.548 --> 00:06:11.290
"This treasure, as well as other shipwrecks that I've marked on a map, are the source of my

95
00:06:11.330 --> 00:06:13.350
great wealth," Captain Nemo explained.

96
00:06:13.370 --> 00:06:16.191
"But doesn't this all belong to someone?"

97
00:06:16.631 --> 00:06:17.011
I asked.

98
00:06:18.012 --> 00:06:18.812
"The Spanish government?

99
00:06:19.260 --> 00:06:22.922
And even if it doesn't, aren't there poor people who could benefit from all these riches?"

100
00:06:23.943 --> 00:06:26.825
As soon as I spoke, I saw that I had wounded the captain.

101
00:06:26.845 --> 00:06:29.846
"Do you think I collect these treasures for myself?"

102
00:06:30.327 --> 00:06:31.147
asked Captain Nemo.

103
00:06:31.167 --> 00:06:37.171
"Do you think I'm ignorant of all the suffering people on this earth, miserable creatures who should be

104
00:06:37.211 --> 00:06:39.832
consoled, victims who should be avenged?"

105
00:06:40.953 --> 00:06:45.596
And then I understood for whom those gold bars in the Grecian islands were destined.

106
00:06:46.676 --> 00:06:52.223
My ruined escape plans seemed like such a small problem compared to the misery of those people who

107
00:06:52.343 --> 00:06:53.784
needed the captain's philanthropy.
